1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,822
Can you show me capstone:

2
00:00:06,822 --> 00:00:09,329
a production-style application?

3
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:12,699
The whole course in one application.

4
00:00:12,699 --> 00:00:16,319
Everything you learned, used once, on purpose:

5
00:00:16,319 --> 00:00:22,221
a frontend, a backend API and a PostgreSQL database in their own namespace,

6
00:00:22,221 --> 00:00:27,729
reachable through an Ingress, configured with ConfigMaps and a Secret,

7
00:00:27,729 --> 00:00:31,506
persistent, health-checked, limited, autoscaled,

8
00:00:31,506 --> 00:00:37,250
backed up every night, isolated by NetworkPolicies and protected by RBAC.

9
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:40,949
You deploy it, inspect it, access it, scale it,

10
00:00:40,949 --> 00:00:45,906
update it, roll it back, and then break it six ways and fix it.

11
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:49,236
Each lesson taught one object in isolation.

12
00:00:49,236 --> 00:00:53,680
Real applications are made of many objects that depend on each other:

13
00:00:53,680 --> 00:01:02,310
a Service that selects the wrong label, a ConfigMap with one wrong value or a NetworkPolicy that is too strict breaks the whole chain,

14
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:04,822
and the symptom appears somewhere else.

15
00:01:04,822 --> 00:01:06,948
The capstone trains exactly that:

16
00:01:06,948 --> 00:01:10,361
following a symptom through the objects to its cause.

17
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,519
And how does it actually work?

18
00:01:15,067 --> 00:01:24,072
| Concept | Where in the capstone | Lesson | |---|---|---| | Namespace | learning-app | 05 | | Deployments,

19
00:01:24,072 --> 00:01:30,889
ReplicaSets, Pods, labels, selectors | frontend and backend | 06–09 | | Services,

20
00:01:30,889 --> 00:01:41,578
DNS | frontend, backend, headless db; the frontend proxies to http://backend:8080 | 10 | | ConfigMaps | backend settings (env),

21
00:01:41,578 --> 00:01:47,975
frontend nginx.conf and page (files) | 11 | | Secret | database credentials,

22
00:01:47,975 --> 00:01:52,351
the password mounted as a file | 12 | | StatefulSet,

23
00:01:52,351 --> 00:02:00,347
PVC, StorageClass | PostgreSQL with a 1 Gi volume from standard; backups on their own PVC | 13,

24
00:02:00,347 --> 00:02:11,372
19 | | Probes | startup, liveness and readiness on every container | 14 | | Requests and limits | every container | 15 | | CronJob,

25
00:02:11,372 --> 00:02:18,189
Job | nightly pg_dump, started by hand with kubectl create job --from=cronjob/...

26
00:02:18,189 --> 00:02:33,675
| 17 | | Ingress | learning-app.local → frontend | 20 | | NetworkPolicies | default deny; ingress → frontend → backend → database ← backup | 22 | | ServiceAccounts | one per component,

27
00:02:33,675 --> 00:02:39,146
no API token mounted | 23 | | RBAC | the user developer may view,

28
00:02:39,146 --> 00:02:43,102
not change | 24 | | Rolling updates, rollbacks,

29
00:02:43,102 --> 00:02:48,488
scaling, HPA | backend 1.0.0 → 2.0.0 → 1.0.0; HPA 2–5 Pods | 09,

30
00:02:48,488 --> 00:02:54,211
25, 26 | | Helm | the same application from the course chart | 28 |.

31
00:02:55,967 --> 00:02:56,127
2.

32
00:02:56,127 --> 00:03:03,981
Deploy, in dependency order (the namespace first, then identities and data, then the application):

33
00:03:04,533 --> 00:03:04,710
3.

34
00:03:04,710 --> 00:03:07,453
Wait until every part is ready:

35
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:12,109
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

36
00:03:12,667 --> 00:03:14,702
The chain, object by object:

37
00:03:14,702 --> 00:03:22,626
the Ingress points at the frontend Service, the Services have endpoints, the backend reads its configuration:

38
00:03:24,367 --> 00:03:26,363
Let me try something.

39
00:03:26,363 --> 00:03:29,784
Six realistic breaks, one at a time:

40
00:03:29,784 --> 00:03:35,106
break, look at the symptom, find the cause, fix, verify.

41
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:37,770
The six breaks, side by side:

42
00:03:37,770 --> 00:03:43,934
the symptom points at an object, the object's events or configuration name the cause.

43
00:03:43,934 --> 00:03:54,812
| Break | What the user saw | Object that explained it | Root cause | |---|---|---|---| | Image | nothing (old Pods kept serving) | Deployment events:

44
00:03:54,812 --> 00:04:00,904
ImagePullBackOff | tag 1.0.1 does not exist | | Service | 502 Bad Gateway | Service:

45
00:04:00,904 --> 00:04:10,404
no endpoints, selector vs labels | selector backend-api | | Configuration | nothing at first; the rollout never finished | new Pod:

46
00:04:10,404 --> 00:04:12,725
Readiness probe failed (/readyz:

47
00:04:12,725 --> 00:04:19,760
database unavailable) | DB_HOST=database | | Probe | nothing (old Pod kept serving) | Pod events:

48
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:26,649
probe on :9090 refused | wrong probe port | | Storage | backups silently not made | PVC events:

49
00:04:26,649 --> 00:04:33,974
no StorageClass fast-ssd | wrong StorageClass | | NetworkPolicy | 504 Gateway Time-out | policy From:

50
00:04:33,974 --> 00:04:35,932
vs the Pods' labels | tier:

51
00:04:35,932 --> 00:04:38,470
front | The pattern behind all six:

52
00:04:38,470 --> 00:04:45,867
user → Ingress → Service → endpoints → Pods → events → logs → configuration → dependencies → policies,

53
00:04:45,867 --> 00:04:50,001
and the habit of checking each layer instead of guessing.

54
00:04:51,767 --> 00:04:57,992
Every fix above applied a reviewed file from capstone/, never a hand-made patch.

55
00:04:57,992 --> 00:05:03,361
Verify the whole application once more, as the user, and the backups:

56
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:07,829
A typo in the version of a release:

57
00:05:08,367 --> 00:05:09,396
Investigation:

58
00:05:09,396 --> 00:05:15,201
the new Pod cannot get its image; the events say why, and the rollout is stuck:

59
00:05:15,767 --> 00:05:17,234
Root cause:

60
00:05:17,234 --> 00:05:21,103
the tag 1.0.1 does not exist.

61
00:05:21,103 --> 00:05:21,637
Fix:

62
00:05:21,637 --> 00:05:22,971
roll back.

63
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:27,308
A selector that matches nothing:

64
00:05:27,867 --> 00:05:28,933
Investigation:

65
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:35,785
the frontend answers (the error comes from nginx), the backend Pods are Running and ready.

66
00:05:35,785 --> 00:05:38,831
Does the backend Service have endpoints?

67
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:40,301
Root cause:

68
00:05:40,301 --> 00:05:47,183
no endpoints, because the selector backend-api matches no Pod (they are backend).

69
00:05:47,183 --> 00:05:47,523
Fix:

70
00:05:47,523 --> 00:05:50,326
apply the reviewed Service again.

71
00:05:52,067 --> 00:05:57,961
The database host name is changed in the ConfigMap, and the backend restarted to pick it up:

72
00:05:58,500 --> 00:05:59,443
Root cause:

73
00:05:59,443 --> 00:06:04,418
DB_HOST=database, and no Service has that name (it is db).

74
00:06:04,418 --> 00:06:04,761
Fix:

75
00:06:04,761 --> 00:06:12,909
the reviewed ConfigMap, and a restart (environment variables are read when a container starts):

76
00:06:14,667 --> 00:06:18,516
The frontend's readiness probe is pointed at the wrong port:

77
00:06:19,067 --> 00:06:20,182
Investigation:

78
00:06:20,182 --> 00:06:24,641
the new Pod runs but is never ready; its events say why:

79
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:26,464
Root cause:

80
00:06:26,464 --> 00:06:31,863
nothing listens on 9090; nginx listens on 8080.

81
00:06:31,863 --> 00:06:32,322
Fix:

82
00:06:32,322 --> 00:06:35,079
the reviewed Deployment.

83
00:06:36,833 --> 00:06:43,577
The backup volume is recreated with a StorageClass that does not exist, then a backup runs.

84
00:06:43,577 --> 00:06:50,098
Kubernetes refuses to remove a claim that a Pod still uses (the claim stays Terminating,

85
00:06:50,098 --> 00:06:54,322
protected by the finalizer kubernetes.io/pvc-protection),

86
00:06:54,322 --> 00:06:59,213
so the finished backup Jobs, whose Pods use it, are deleted first:

87
00:06:59,767 --> 00:07:00,821
Investigation:

88
00:07:00,821 --> 00:07:05,716
the Pod waits for its volume, the volume waits for a provisioner:

89
00:07:06,267 --> 00:07:07,279
Root cause:

90
00:07:07,279 --> 00:07:12,062
StorageClass fast-ssd does not exist; only standard.

91
00:07:12,062 --> 00:07:15,374
A PVC's class cannot be changed, so:

92
00:07:15,374 --> 00:07:22,366
delete the broken Job and PVC, apply the reviewed PVC, run the backup again.

93
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:28,334
A "tidy-up" of the backend policy changes the label it allows:

94
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:30,879
Follow the evidence.

95
00:07:31,433 --> 00:07:32,329
Root cause:

96
00:07:32,329 --> 00:07:35,344
the policy allows Pods labelled tier:

97
00:07:35,344 --> 00:07:38,114
front; the frontend Pods are tier:

98
00:07:38,114 --> 00:07:41,210
frontend, so the default deny applies.

99
00:07:41,210 --> 00:07:41,536
Fix:

100
00:07:41,536 --> 00:07:43,328
the reviewed policies.

101
00:07:45,067 --> 00:07:45,780
Got it.

102
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:48,531
What do I need to remember?

103
00:07:49,067 --> 00:07:54,801
A working application is a chain of objects; a break anywhere shows up at the user.

104
00:07:55,367 --> 00:07:57,740
Troubleshoot from the outside in:

105
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:03,351
user → Ingress → Service → endpoints → Pods → events → logs → configuration →.

106
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:08,156
Fix the reviewed files and apply them; verify as the user,

107
00:08:08,156 --> 00:08:12,044
then check the parts the user does not see (backups).
